I can do that. Here's a dark mode theme that preserves the visual hierarchy I've used in the original design (ERG0487):

```css
/* dark mode */
body {
  background: #000;
  color: #fff;
}
header {
  background: #111;
  border-bottom: 1px solid #333;
}
header .brand {
  color: #fff;
}
header .brand span {
  color: #00f;
}
header .nav a {
  color: #aaa;
  text-decoration: none;
}
header .nav a:hover {
  color: #fff;
}
nav {
  background: #111;
  border-right: 1px solid #333;
  color: #eee;
}
nav .section {
  background: #222;
  color: #ddd;
  border-bottom: 1px solid #444;
}
nav .section.active, nav .section:hover {
  background: #333;
  color: #fff;
}
main {
  background: #111;
  color: #eee;
}
main .content {
  background: #222;
  color: #fff;
  border: 1px solid #444;
}
main .content h3 {
  color: #00f;
}
main .content p {
  color: #ccc;
}
main .content code {
  background: #333;
  color: #0f0;
  border: 1px solid #555;
}
main .content ul {
  color: #ccc;
}
main .content ul li::before {
  color: #00f;
}
main .content .highlight {
  background: #333;
  border: 1px solid #00f;
  color: #00f;
}
footer {
  background: #111;
  color: #aaa;
  border-top: 1px solid #333;
}
footer a {
  color: #00f;
}
```

A few notes on implementation:

- I've kept the blue accent (#00f) to maintain the visual identity on the dark background
- I used `#111` for the primary gray background instead of pure black to prevent visual fatigue
- I use `#333` as the secondary gray for navigation items when active or hovered
